Do you want homeomorphism classes or actual different topologies?
Let's count both with the set X={1,2,3}:
1) The indiscrete topology: {{},X}
2) The discrete topology: all subsets.
3) one open set with one point: {{},{1},X}
 There are three of these.
4) One singleton open, the complement open also:
 {{},{1},{2,3},X}
 There are three of these.
5) one singleton open, one pair with element open.
 {{},{1},{1,2},X}
 There are six of these.
6) One singleton open, both pairs with that element open:
 {{},{1},{1,2},{1,3},X}
 There are three of these.
7) Two different singletons open, one pair open:
 {{},{1},{2},{1,2},X}
 There are three of these.
8) Two different singletons open, two pair open
 {{},{1},{2},{1,2},{1,3},X}
 There are three of these.
9) One open set with two points: {{},{1,2},X}
 Again, three of these.

Now, if all three singletons are open, the topology is disrete. If all three pairs are open, all singletons are also, so the topology is discrete(2). If two pairs are open, their intersection singleton is open. In this case, one other singleton may be open(6,8). If only one pair is open, then one or both singletons in that pair may be open(5,7), or the complement can be open(4), or no singleton may be open(9). If no pairs are open, one singleton may be(3), or none may be (1).
